  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The rule was created enabled and reads back as enabled, so no enable call was needed.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (`d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`) has 27 fields, including `Carrier` (string), `Cancelled` (boolean), `DistanceKilometers` (number), and `timestamp` (date). Its time field is `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-9cb19b3c Flights' (`9d5fe634-49d7-4662-ae59-88c72efdc5d2`) has two panels, both on the flights data view by reference:\n   - A line chart of count over `timestamp` by date histogram.\n   - A bar chart of count by `Carrier` terms. I created it as 'Carrier breakdown' and renamed it in step 4.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-9cb19b3c Cancelled flights' (`0bf11c84-7e05-4da1-92b6-4726c9f6d690`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on the flights data view. It filters on `Cancelled : true` (KQL), runs every 1m, and fires when the count over a 5m window is greater than 10. I used the `alerts` consumer.\n4. **Edit and read-back:** The carrier panel is now titled 'Flights by carrier'. Reading the rule back shows `enabled=True`, schedule `1m`, last execution status `ok`.\n\nI did not run the rule's condition against the data, so I haven't confirmed that any cancelled-flight window actually crosses the threshold.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
